{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00471 - MONDO_grouped_11786_5324_24332",
  "verbalization": "Montelukast, a leukotriene receptor antagonist, is implicated in the treatment of allergic rhinitis through its ability to inhibit the action of leukotrienes, which are mediators involved in allergic inflammation. The visible evidence suggests that Montelukast may be repurposed for allergic rhinitis through a chain of indications that ultimately lead to the condition. Specifically, Montelukast is indicated for vasomotor rhinitis, which is a common form of allergic rhinitis characterized by nasal congestion and rhinorrhea triggered by environmental stimuli. Vasomotor rhinitis is further indicated for mebhydrolin and mizolastine, both of which are also leukotriene receptor antagonists. The presence of these indications, along with the direct indication for vasomotor rhinitis, provides a plausible pathway for Montelukast to be considered for the treatment of allergic rhinitis. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Montelukast and allergic rhinitis through the inhibition of leukotriene-mediated inflammation, although the exact role of each intermediate step in the chain remains to be fully elucidated.",
